/**
 * Queueable job that creates a CSAT-survey Task for a single closed Case.
 * Each case is handled by its own job instance so a failure on one case
 * cannot roll back or block the work on any other case.
 */
public class CaseSurveyJob implements System.Queueable {

    private final Id caseId;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseId = caseId;
    }

    public void execute(System.QueueableContext ctx) {
        Case c = [SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId FROM Case WHERE Id = :caseId LIMIT 1];
        if (c == null || c.ContactId == null) {
            return;
        }
        Task t = new Task(
            WhatId       = c.Id,
            WhoId        = c.ContactId,
            Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
            ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
            Status       = 'Not Started'
        );
        insert t;
    }

    /**
     * Inner Queueable that dispatches a list of case-ids into individual
     * CaseSurveyJob enqueues while respecting the platform limit of
     * 50 System.enqueueJob calls per execute context.  When more than 50
     * cases exist, it enqueues up to 49 case-jobs and chains one more
     * Dispatcher with the remaining ids.
     */
    public class Dispatcher implements System.Queueable {

        private static final Integer MAX_PER_CONTEXT = 50;

        private final List<Id> caseIds;

        public Dispatcher(List<Id> caseIds) {
            this.caseIds = caseIds;
        }

        public void execute(System.QueueableContext ctx) {
            Integer total = caseIds.size();
            if (total <= MAX_PER_CONTEXT) {
                for (Id cid : caseIds) {
                    System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(cid));
                }
            } else {
                // Reserve one enqueue slot for the next Dispatcher chunk
                for (Integer i = 0; i < MAX_PER_CONTEXT - 1; i++) {
                    System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(caseIds[i]));
                }
                List<Id> remaining = new List<Id>();
                for (Integer i = MAX_PER_CONTEXT - 1; i < total; i++) {
                    remaining.add(caseIds[i]);
                }
                System.enqueueJob(new Dispatcher(remaining));
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**What changed and why**

| Change | Reason |
|---|---|
| Trigger no longer calls `System.enqueueJob` inside the `for` loop. It collects the newly-closed case Ids into a list and enqueues a **single** `Dispatcher`. | Salesforce hard-limits `System.enqueueJob` to **50 calls per execute context**. Closing 200 cases in one transaction would throw the exact `LimitException` seen in the failing test. |
| `CaseSurveyJob.Dispatcher` (inner class) chunks the work. | Each `execute` makes at most 50 `System.enqueueJob` calls (49 case-jobs + 1 chained Dispatcher, or ≤50 when the remainder is small enough). This keeps the "one job per case → one failure can't affect others" guarantee while staying within the platform limit. |
| Defensive re-query of the Case inside `CaseSurveyJob.execute`. | The Contact may have been reassigned between trigger execution and async execution; the guard avoids a DML failure. |

The `sendEmail`-inside-the-loop approach the user specified is fundamentally incompatible with Apex governor limits: the limit is **10 email invocations per synchronous transaction**, and each `Messaging.sendEmail(...)` call counts as one invocation regardless of how many messages it contains. With 200 opportunities, that's 200 invocations → immediate `LimitException`. I should have flagged this as the major issue rather than just the efficiency concern.

The correct approach is to **build all messages first, then make a single `Messaging.sendEmail` call** with the full list. Each `SendEmailResult` is independent, so a failure for one opportunity doesn't prevent the others from sending — which satisfies the fault-isolation requirement without violating governor limits.

